  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers this morning, along with gusty winds. \nIsolated power outages possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCold front and an upper trough swings through the region early \nmorning. Across northern portions of the forecast area, showers \nwill accompany the cold front and upper level trough, gradually \ntapering out of the region by mid-morning. Rainfall with this \nsystem will have minimal impact on the drought.\n\nA chance for winds gusts 25-35mph is possible today, as gusty \nwinds could mix down to the surface. Winds increase this \nmorning, and stay elevated into the late afternoon. Potential \nfor isolated power outages with elevated gusts. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy/areas of frost possible across\nnorthern areas tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region tonight, bringing \nclear skies and light winds. This will result in favorable \nconditions for radiational cooling. Low temperatures will \ngenerally range from the mid to upper 30s across much of \nnorthern Maine. However, temperatures could locally drop to \naround freezing across northwest areas. Patchy/areas of frost \nare possible tonight across northern areas, particularly in the\nnormally colder low lying locations. Patchy river valley fog is\nalso possible later tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance for rain showers by late next week,\nthough little to no drought improvement anticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system to the far north moves eastward, pushing a \nfront through New England late next week. Models are coming into\nbetter agreement on the timing of the front, likely Thursday. \nModels indicate the front will likely be relatively weak, \nbringing only rain showers. Not anticipating enough rainfall to \nprovide drought improvement at this time.",
